selfcheck ok: dial_table parses the real cage-tier.yaml; UNCAGED sits at the top of the permissiveness lattice, every real tier below it, tighter tiers below looser ones; a dial tightening classifies major and names the moved corpus entries and the dial CEL expression (never an enumerated dial list); a widen-only change is patch; no change is none; one field regressing while another improves is still major (spec-level, not dial-level); priorityClass ranks on the ACTUAL assigned PriorityClass's real value, so a tier's pc field repointed at a different PriorityClass registers as major even with every other dial untouched (cs-21 regression guard); cage-tier added/removed fall out of the same per-pod comparison with no special case; the major detail is one sentence with no viability language; Track 1 reuses rederive_bumps.classify_policy unchanged; classify_repo reproduces the three known-good bumps; a whole-body release whose only movement is a policy removal computes patch; cs-16: a self-scoped pair (real render-version-tree.py bodies, real generated corpus, real kyverno apply) differing ONLY in the version literal classifies 'none', never the old spurious 'major' the vacuous self-scope match used to produce, and a real narrowing bundled with the same version bump still classifies 'major' -- the fix removes the artifact without suppressing real movement
